    "en":  {
               "headline":  "Record Q3 overshadowed by China-firmware short report; AMD breaks below 200-day EMA",
               "summary":  "AMD\u0027s business is accelerating. Q3 2026 revenue reached $13.4B (+45%), Data Center revenue hit a record $8.5B (+97%), and Q4 guidance of about $17B beat the roughly $16.2B consensus. The stock went the other way. A Hunterbrook short report on November 19 linked AMD firmware to IEIT Systems, a blacklisted Chinese server maker. That came on top of a US export rule on November 16 and an AI-chip selloff across Asia. AMD closed at $424.39 on November 20, down 13.25% that day, below all three EMAs and about 36% under its $658.52 high.",
               "pros":  [
                            "Q4 2026 guidance of about $17B (±3%) implies roughly 27% growth on Q3\u0027s $13.4B.",
                            "Anthropic, Meta, Microsoft, OpenAI and Oracle are already running models on the first Helios MI450 racks.",
                            "Data Center was 64% of Q3 revenue and grew 97% year on year, with a 34% operating margin.",
                            "Lisa Su expects server revenue to double in 2027, and several analysts model about 80% total growth for 2027.",
                            "Hunterbrook says itself that its findings do not show AMD broke export law."
                        ],
               "cons":  [
                            "Price fell under the EMA 200 ($445.14) on 1.65× normal volume, which is a high-conviction breakdown.",
                            "Hunterbrook alleges IEIT shipped at least six newer AMD firmware versions after the March 2025 blacklisting, and law firms opened securities probes on November 20.",
                            "The November 16 Commerce rule requires licences for AI chips sent to Singapore, Malaysia, Thailand, Indonesia and Brazil.",
                            "In FY2025, 22.4% of revenue was billed to China and Hong Kong and 12.4% to Singapore, so regulatory headlines hit hard.",
                            "Gross margin guidance of about 55% non-GAAP is far below NVIDIA\u0027s, which suggests AMD still competes partly on price."
                        ],
               "opportunityCheck":  "The scores look mostly reasonable. I would raise \u0027position\u0027 by one or two points. Q3 showed paying, at-scale Helios deployments with every major AI lab and three hyperscalers, and Citi says supply, not demand, is now the main constraint. That is stronger than a distant number two. I would keep \u0027bottleneck \u0026 pricing power\u0027 where it is: a 55% gross margin guide shows AMD is not setting prices. If the IEIT allegations lead to a formal Commerce finding, trim \u0027optionality\u0027. Nothing found so far justifies that.",
               "technical":  "Trend: price ($424.39) is below the EMA 50 ($526.11), EMA 100 ($508.96) and EMA 200 ($445.14). EMAs are exponential moving averages of price over 50, 100 and 200 days. They are still stacked bullishly (50 \u003e 100 \u003e 200), so the long uptrend has not formally reversed. But losing the 200-day line on November 20 is a major warning. Momentum: MACD (a trend-momentum gauge) is -16.29, below its signal line at -12.82, and the histogram is negative at -3.47, so downside momentum is still building. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge; under 30 is oversold) is 38.42: weak, but not yet oversold. Volatility: price is just above the lower Bollinger Band at $414.13 (bands mark roughly two standard deviations around the 20-day average of $509.08), so the move is stretched. It sits about halfway in the 52-week range of $188.22–$658.52. Volume of 70.0M shares was 1.65× the 20-day average, and the day closed near its $418.76 low after opening at $471.73. Sellers were in control. Support is at $414–419. Resistance is at $445, then about $509.",
               "fundamental":  "AMD is the main merchant alternative to NVIDIA for AI accelerators and leads in server CPUs. Q3 2026, reported November 3, was a clear beat. Revenue was $13.38B against $13.09B expected, and non-GAAP EPS was $2.09 against $1.95. Data Center revenue was $8.5B, up 97%, driven by EPYC CPUs and the MI450 launch. Q4 guidance is about $17B with a roughly 55% non-GAAP gross margin. Weak spots: Gaming fell 40%, and margins came in slightly under the Street\u0027s expectations. Analysts on November 4 mostly stayed at Buy or Overweight. Morgan Stanley (Equal-weight, $550) and JPMorgan (Neutral, $575) were more cautious. The moat thesis rests on the Helios ramp in 2027. The fundamentals reinforce it. The open question is regulatory, not about demand.",
               "news":  [
                            "November 3: Q3 revenue was $13.4B (+45% YoY) and Data Center revenue was $8.5B (+97%). Q4 guidance is about $17B ±3%, and Lisa Su said growth would accelerate as Helios and \u0027Venice\u0027 EPYC production scales.",
                            "November 4: Citi ($800), UBS ($700), Goldman ($675) and Barclays ($665) kept bullish ratings. Some trimmed price targets, and Barclays flagged slightly soft gross margins.",
                            "November 16: Commerce\u0027s BIS issued a rule requiring licences for advanced AI chips going to data centres in Singapore, Malaysia, Thailand, Indonesia and Brazil. The rule targets Chinese remote access to the chips and names both NVIDIA and AMD parts.",
                            "November 19: Hunterbrook, which is short AMD, reported that blacklisted IEIT Systems kept shipping newer AMD firmware, most recently on October 19. It also said Aivres, an AMD Instinct partner, offers near-identical firmware. AMD reportedly rejected the report and said it complies with export rules.",
                            "November 19–20: AMD fell 7.7% and then 13.25%, a loss of more than 24% over five sessions. Taiwan\u0027s Taiex had its worst week since 1991. Several law firms announced investigations into possible securities claims."
                        ],
               "caution":  "The breakdown happened on heavy volume and below the 200-day EMA, and MACD is still falling. Short-term sellers clearly have control. The IEIT issue may not fade quickly. If Commerce opens an inquiry or forces changes at partners such as Aivres, Helios server supply and China-linked revenue could both suffer. Class-action probes tend to keep headlines going for months. Hunterbrook\u0027s affiliated fund profits if the stock falls, so treat its framing critically. But AMD has not given a detailed public rebuttal yet.",
               "opportunity":  "None of the November news touches AMD\u0027s order book. Q4 guidance is about 27% above Q3, five leading AI buyers already run Helios, and supply is the main constraint. Hunterbrook concedes it has not shown a legal violation. At $424.39, price sits just above the lower Bollinger Band ($414.13), and RSI 38 is near oversold. For long-term holders, the main question is whether $414–445 holds as a base. Watch for any Commerce response to the report.",
               "risks":  [
                             "Commerce enforcement or licence restrictions could follow from the IEIT firmware allegations.",
                             "Securities class actions filed after the November 19–20 drop could weigh on the stock.",
                             "New licence rules for Southeast Asia and Brazil (November 16) may slow non-US data-centre sales.",
                             "Any slip in the MI450/MI455 Helios ramp would hurt, because Q4 and 2027 guidance assume a steep ramp.",
                             "The AI-capex sentiment reversal visible across Asian chip stocks may continue."
                         ]
